# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Rova — LOCAL DEVELOPMENT environment
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# From a clean clone, `cp .env.example .env` is the ONLY setup this file needs. Every value below boots the API, the
# worker and the web dev server against `docker compose -f docker-compose.dev.yml up -d` (README → "Local stack").
# `test/env-example.spec.ts` parses this file against the real schema and the dev tooling on every CI run, and the
# "local quickstart" job boots the stack from it, so it cannot drift quietly.
#
# SECRETS HERE ARE LOCAL PLACEHOLDERS (`local-dev-only-…`, and one throwaway signing key), not secrets. Never paste a
# real or deployed value into this file or into `.env`. To rotate a local one:  openssl rand -base64 48
#
# WHO READS WHAT. The API and the worker validate against ONE schema (libs/platform/src/config/env.schema.ts), so a
# variable one of them does not use is simply ignored by it and nothing is refused:
#   [both]    read by the API and the worker (the schema requires it in each).
#   [API]     only the API reads it.
#   [WORKER]  only the worker reads it.
#   [web]     the Vite dev server; Vite exposes ONLY `VITE_`-prefixed variables to the browser.
#
# PORTS (the defaults; opshub's stack is offset from these so both run at once, see README → "Local stack"):
#   API 3000 · web 5173 · Postgres 5432 · Valkey 6379 · LocalStack 4566 · OTel collector 4318 (metrics 8889)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Compose (docker-compose.dev.yml) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read by `docker compose` only; the app never reads these. Each host port is bound to 127.0.0.1.
# A second copy of this repo (README → "Run a second copy") sets COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME and these five, AND the
# matching URLs further down: DATABASE_URL, REDIS_URL, STORAGE_ENDPOINT, AWS_ENDPOINT_URL, OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT.
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=rova
POSTGRES_HOST_PORT=5432
VALKEY_HOST_PORT=6379
LOCALSTACK_HOST_PORT=4566
OTEL_OTLP_HOST_PORT=4318
OTEL_METRICS_HOST_PORT=8889

# ── Process ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [both] `development` is what allows the passwordless dev-login; `production` hard-blocks it.
NODE_ENV=development
# [API] The port apps/web/vite.config.ts proxies /v1 to (API_TARGET there overrides it).
PORT=3000
HOST=0.0.0.0
# [API] The Vite dev server's origin (CORS allow-list).
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173
# [both] The origin the BROWSER uses: the SPA. Used in email links and invitation URLs.
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5173
# [API] Set to 'true' to bypass all rate limiting. Never in a deployed environment.
DISABLE_RATE_LIMIT=false
# [API] Serve Swagger UI and the OpenAPI document at /api/docs. Never enable in a deployed environment.
SWAGGER_ENABLED=true

# ── Database (PostgreSQL) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [both] The compose stack's superuser (postgres/postgres), database `rova_dev`, on POSTGRES_HOST_PORT (5432). A fresh
# database has no login for the app's least-privilege roles (`rova_app`, `rova_worker`, `rova_migrate`: migration 0068
# creates them NOLOGIN), so the zero-setup default is the superuser. To run as the restricted role the way CI does:
#   ALTER ROLE rova_app LOGIN PASSWORD 'rova_app';  then point DATABASE_URL at it (docs/runbooks/db-role-least-privilege.md)
# Deployed environments set DATABASE_HOST/PORT/NAME as plain env and DATABASE_USER/PASSWORD from the RDS-managed secret
# instead (db/database-url.ts).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/rova_dev?sslmode=disable
DATABASE_POOL_MIN=2
DATABASE_POOL_MAX=20
# Owner connection for DDL (`pnpm db:migrate`, db/truncate-all.ts); never used by the running app. Falls back to
# DATABASE_URL when unset, which is what you want locally.
# DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/rova_dev?sslmode=disable
# Demo fixtures: `pnpm db:migrate` with this on also loads the demo users (admin@qnsc.dev, dev@qnsc.dev,
# viewer@qnsc.dev) and one sample project, so the dev-login below has an account to sign in as. Refused by the migrator
# whenever NODE_ENV=production.
SEED_ON_DEPLOY=true
# The first admin is NOT a seeded password: in a deployed environment their Entra email goes here and they are
# auto-granted workspace_admin on first SSO login (comma-separated). Locally, admin@qnsc.dev is seeded.
PLATFORM_ADMIN_EMAILS=
# BOOTSTRAP_WORKSPACE_NAME=ACME Corp
# BOOTSTRAP_WORKSPACE_SLUG=main

# ── Cache (Valkey / Redis) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [both] The compose stack's Valkey, on VALKEY_HOST_PORT (6379; opshub's is offset to 6380).
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
REDIS_KEY_PREFIX=rova:

# ── Auth: identity 8 (Better Auth, the `staff` preset, Microsoft Entra) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Sign-in sets an opaque, httpOnly `__Secure-rova.session_token` cookie; the browser never holds a token. The SPA and the
# API must be same-origin, which the Vite proxy provides locally (/v1 and /api/auth) and the Cloudflare Pages Function
# deployed.
#
# [both] Signs fastify-cookie. 32+ characters.
COOKIE_SECRET=local-dev-only-cookie-secret-0123456789abcdef
# [both] HMAC key binding a CSRF token to its session. Distinct from COOKIE_SECRET on purpose (rotating one must not
# read as a change to the other). 32+ characters.
CSRF_SECRET=local-dev-only-csrf-secret-0123456789abcdefgh
# [API] Signs identity's cookies and encrypts what it stores. 32+ characters, its OWN secret (not COOKIE_SECRET, not
# CSRF_SECRET). Rotating it signs everyone out. The worker is not given it. Throwaway local value:
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=local-dev-only-better-auth-secret-0123456789abcdef

# ── Sign in locally: test-login ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [API] `enabled` loads identity's passwordless POST /api/auth/test-login (a seeded email, no Entra). Identity refuses it
# unless NODE_ENV is `test` or `development`, and the env schema refuses it in production.
IDENTITY_TEST_LOGIN=enabled
# [web] Shows the "dev login" form on the login page, backed by test-login. In the dev server only: `vite build` and the
# unit tests never read this file.
VITE_DEV_LOGIN=true

# ── Microsoft Entra ID (the schema REQUIRES these three in every environment) ────────────────────────────────────
# [both] The API refuses to boot without them, so the values below are PLACEHOLDERS that let it start: they sign
# nobody in. test-login needs nothing else. To sign in with Microsoft locally, register a DEV app in a tenant you control
# (a separate registration from production's): platform *Web*, redirect URI  http://localhost:5173/api/auth/callback/microsoft ,
# delegated openid/profile/email, a client secret; put the real tenant id, client id and secret here and restart.
# The redirect is on the SPA origin because the browser lands there and the session cookie is set on that response.
ENTRA_TENANT_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
ENTRA_CLIENT_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET=local-dev-only-not-a-secret
# [API] The key the Cloudflare Pages Function signs the visitor's address with, verified by `clientIp()`. Leave unset
# locally (no Function in front: the address is the socket's). >= 32 characters when set. docs/runbooks/edge-client-ip.md
# EDGE_CLIENT_IP_SECRET=
# EDGE_CLIENT_IP_SECRET_PREVIOUS=
# Staff email domains (comma-separated, exact; `*.x.y` opts into sub-domains). identity refuses a Microsoft sign-in
# whose address is outside them (B2B guests excepted). Default qnsc.vn.
# SSO_ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAINS=qnsc.vn
# Whether a staff address may create its own account at first sign-in. Deployed environments run invite-only (false).
# SSO_JIT_ENABLED=true
# Provision an invited external collaborator as an Entra B2B GUEST (reuses the registration above; needs the
# `User.Invite.All` APPLICATION permission with admin consent). Off = no guest_invite_outbox row.
# ENTRA_GUEST_INVITE_ENABLED=false
# Email domains INTERNAL to this deployment's tenant (comma-separated, exact), which skip the guest queue and also get
# the copy-link invitation path. Empty = every address is external.
# INTERNAL_EMAIL_DOMAINS=qnsc.vn

# ── Object storage (S3 → LocalStack locally) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [both] LocalStack is the compose stack's S3 on LOCALSTACK_HOST_PORT (4566; opshub's is offset to 4567). Its init hook
# (scripts/localstack/01-bootstrap.sh) creates BOTH buckets below with the CORS rules the SPA's direct PUT needs, and
# the compose healthcheck passes only once they exist, so `up -d --wait` returns when uploads can work.
# PRIVATE: every permission-gated upload, served only via presigned GET.
S3_ATTACHMENTS_BUCKET=rova-attachments
# PUBLIC: avatars and workspace logos only, world-readable by key.
S3_PUBLIC_ASSETS_BUCKET=rova-public-assets
# StorageService selects its backend from STORAGE_ENDPOINT, NOT AWS_ENDPOINT_URL. With it UNSET the S3 client talks to
# real AWS with the task role, which fails on a laptop (no credentials) and would reach a real bucket. `test`/`test` are
# LocalStack's fixed fake credentials; path-style addressing is required by LocalStack and R2 alike.
STORAGE_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4566
STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=test
STORAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=test
STORAGE_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true
# Optional: credentials scoped to the PUBLIC bucket only (both or neither). Unset: public assets reuse the pair above.
# STORAGE_PUBLIC_ACCESS_KEY_ID=test
# STORAGE_PUBLIC_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=test
# Optional: CDN origin for the PUBLIC bucket only. MUST NEVER point at S3_ATTACHMENTS_BUCKET.
# CDN_PUBLIC_ASSETS_BASE_URL=

# ── AWS (SES, Secrets Manager) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
AWS_REGION=ap-southeast-1
# [both] The AWS SDK clients (SES, Secrets Manager, the bounce queue) and the `aws` CLI use this; the app's S3 goes
# through STORAGE_ENDPOINT above. Fake credentials: LocalStack ignores them but the SDK insists on signing.
AWS_ENDPOINT_URL=http://localhost:4566
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=test
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=test

# ── Email ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [both] EMAIL_PROVIDER=dev logs the message to the console and sends nothing (no setup at all).
#        graph  → Microsoft 365 (Graph sendMail) from the environment's shared mailbox (develop noreply-rova-dev@, production noreply-rova@qnsc.vn). The WORKER sends, as the "Rova <Env> Service" app
#                 (MAIL_GRAPH_CLIENT_ID) with the certificate in MAIL_GRAPH_CERTIFICATE; no Graph permission is granted in Entra, the mailbox is
#                 granted by Exchange RBAC. Setup: docs/runbooks/mail-graph.md.
#        ses    → AWS SES (being replaced by graph). Locally, through LocalStack, after verifying the sender once:
#                 docker exec -i rova-localstack awslocal ses verify-email-identity --email-address noreply@qnsc.vn
#        resend → Resend (set RESEND_API_KEY).
# Any non-dev provider refuses to boot without MAIL_FROM_EMAIL. The address is the one deployed environments use, on
# our own domain; with the dev provider it is only shown in the log.
EMAIL_PROVIDER=dev
# graph only: the mailbox mail goes out as (required), the worker's credential (PEM key + certificate, raw or base64) and, later on k3s, the token file.
# MAIL_GRAPH_SENDER=noreply-rova-dev@qnsc.vn   # develop; production is noreply-rova@qnsc.vn
# MAIL_GRAPH_CLIENT_ID=      # the "Rova <Env> Service" Entra app (certificate only), not ENTRA_CLIENT_ID
# MAIL_GRAPH_CERTIFICATE=
# MAIL_GRAPH_FEDERATED_TOKEN_FILE=
MAIL_FROM_EMAIL=noreply@qnsc.vn
# MAIL_FROM_NAME=Mini Rova
# MAIL_REPLY_TO=
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# [WORKER] SES asynchronous feedback: CONFIGSET tags every send so bounce/complaint events reach the worker's
# BounceFeedbackService through SQS; QUEUE_URL is that queue and doubles as the consumer's on/off switch (unset = off).
# SES_BOUNCE_CONFIGSET=rova-email-feedback
# SES_BOUNCE_QUEUE_URL=

# ── Observability ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SERVICE_VERSION=dev
# [both] The DEPLOYMENT environment, distinct from NODE_ENV (which develop pins to `production`): stamped on every log
# line as `env` and on the OTel resource. `local` keeps a laptop's output out of develop's and production's panels.
DEPLOYMENT_ENV=local
LOG_LEVEL=debug
LOG_PRETTY=true
LOG_SQL=false
LOG_HTTP_BODIES=false
LOG_DEV_EMAIL_CONTENT=false
# [both] ON locally because the compose stack ships the collector that receives it (scripts/otel/collector.yaml).
# main.ts loads this file BEFORE the OTel bootstrap (`@platform/config/load-env`), which is why the flag takes effect.
# Spans and metrics land in `docker logs rova-otel`; metrics are readable as Prometheus text with
# `curl -s localhost:8889/metrics`. Set false to run without the compose stack, or the SDK retries a dead socket.
OTEL_ENABLED=true
OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=rova-api
# [WORKER] The worker reports itself under this name so it is not mistaken for the API.
OTEL_WORKER_SERVICE_NAME=rova-worker
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318
# Head-sampling fraction; unset = 1.0 outside production, 0.1 in it.
# OTEL_SAMPLING_PROBABILITY=1.0
# [both] Circuit breakers and bulkheads around outbound calls.
RESILIENCE_ENABLED=true

# ── Optional: GitHub App (SCM integration) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GITHUB_APP_ID=
# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
# GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# GITHUB_API_BASE_URL=https://api.github.com
